it's my great pleasure to  welcome you to this event and conference  at the victorian albert museum  also on behalf of yale university and  its president peter salovey  we have been talking about  organizing a conference like this on  illicit traffic and looting since the  last six  months pretty early on when i joined  yale university last april  martin and me got in touch and thought  that  would be really  a primary aim of the time  to bring experts and professionals  together to discuss this issue we little  could we know that  on april 14  we would be  doing this in an entirely different  context  and we thought about it a half a year  ago  i think for the yale university  which has been a university  since 1701  in new england  new haven connecticut  the first century maybe being a  university for  the american east coast and in the  second century  maybe a university for the whole country  but now  it has clearly global ambitions and  wants to be a global university  and  of course a university like yale with  global ambitions has to lend its voice  has to become an agora for discussions  like this discussions which are  clearly in need of a global and  international response  it's not so much about our voice but  it's really about your voice and i would  like to thank all the speakers and all  the participants who followed our  invitation as martin was saying to come  here to london to the victorian albert  museum  um i mean you are the essential voice in  this debate and to the same extent also  those colleagues who actually as martin  said send video presentations  powerpoints but who could not be here in  person because they are facing  completely different conditions of work  in their daily life in syria in mali  nigeria yemen libya and iraq  we have  together with  my colleague vernon rapley we have  structured the conference in different  sessions from lessons learned from the  past  current concerns on the ground  very important session about working 